  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are probable Monday night \nand Tuesday night \n\nAside from varying amounts of mainly mid level clouds with some\nvirga across our SE zones skies were mainly clear near and to\nthe north of the Interstate 80 corridor with some patchy valley\nfog developing across the Northern Mtns where air/waters delta\nTs were 20 deg F or more. \n\nNoticeably cooler/drier air already filtering into CPA with\ndewpoints in the 40s, to low 50s in the SE. This lead to a \nrefreshingly crisp start to the 1st weekend of October with \nabundant sunshine and low humidity. \n\nThe coldest air of the early Autumn season is poised to arrive \nby early next week with frost/freeze conditions expected Monday \nnight and Tuesday night over much of the forecast area.\nLocations to the northwest of I-81 have the highest odds for\nfrost with min temps around the freezing mark in the northern\ntier. \n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Not much rain expected for the first 10 days of \nOctober \n\nThe cold front that brought clouds and some periods of light \nrain to the region on Friday has become nearly stationary early \ntoday from the Interstate 81 corridor in Virginia to the Delmarva \nCoast. \n\nVariable amounts of clouds will persist across Southern PA today\nwith nothing more than a few light sprinkles possible. Abundant\nsunshine, a 5 to 10 mph North to Northeast wind, fairly low\nhumidity in the 30s and 40s this afternoon, excellent visibility\nand near normal high temperatures will kick off this first weekend\non October across Central and Northern PA. \n\nA fast-moving and amplifying trough moving our way from the  \nNorthern U.S. and South-Central Canada will bring a periods of\nincreased moisture and cloud cover for Sunday into Sunday night\nacross much of Central and Southern PA with a likelihood of \nsome light rain, thanks to periods of modest meso-B uvvel\nassociated with the right entrance region of several jetlets\nmoving over, or just to the north of PA.\n\nThe best chance for light rainfall amounts of several hundredths, \nup to one tenth of an inch will be across portions of the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley and adjacent Southeastern PA. Elsewhere\nacross the Central Ridge and Valley Region of the state, the\nLaurel Highlands and Northern Mtns, there may be just enough\nrain to wet the ground at times but only measure a few\nhundredths of an inch.\n\nOutside of this narrow window for light precip, expect a \nprolonged dry stretch to last through next week as a large area \nof Canadian high pressure dominates the pattern.",
